3. Discussion

The meeting began with an introduction to Week 04, focusing on enhancing the existing PIC16F72 Microcontroller Simulator by dividing it into three independent processes.

The team discussed moving from a traditional single-program approach towards system-level software design, where different components operate independently and communicate through IPC mechanisms.

The proposed architecture consists of three processes:

UI Process – Shayaan: Responsible for user interaction, code input and simulator display.

Core Process – Prakrithi: Responsible for CPU execution, memory management, stack and queue operations.

Logging Process – Sancia: Responsible for recording execution details, errors and relevant system activities.

Mohammed Ayman, as Team Leader, would be responsible for integrating all three processes, resolving IPC and interface issues, performing system testing and conducting benchmarking.

The team also discussed using threads wherever appropriate within individual processes and investigating suitable POSIX IPC mechanisms to enable communication.

Performance evaluation was another important topic. The team agreed to compare the existing standalone simulator with the new multi-process implementation by measuring execution time, IPC overhead, CPU usage and memory consumption.

